+  repa: {
+    title: 'REPA (Representation Alignment)',
+    description: (
+      <>
+        Experimental. REPA adds a second loss to training. A small helper network (the projector) is trained to turn the
+        model's early image features into the features a frozen vision model (DINOv2) sees in the same, clean training
+        image, and the model is pushed to make that easy. The projector is only used while training and is not part of
+        the saved LoRA. Based on "Representation Alignment for Generation: Training Diffusion Transformers Is Easier
+        Than You Think" (Yu et al., ICLR 2025).
+        <br />
+        <br />
+        What it costs: the first run downloads the vision model (about 4.4 GB for the default{' '}
+        <code>facebook/dinov2-giant</code>). If your latents were cached without REPA, they are cached again once,
+        because REPA also needs the exact images the latents were made from. With <b>Cache Latents</b> on in your dataset, the
+        vision model runs once over your images before training, stores its features next to the latents (about 7 MB per
+        768px image) and is then unloaded, so it adds little to training time or VRAM. Without it, the vision model
+        stays on the GPU and runs every step.
+        <br />
+        <br />
+        <b>Weight</b> is how strongly the alignment counts against the normal loss (0.5 by default). <b>Block</b> is
+        which transformer block's output is aligned, counting from 0 (8 by default). The encoder, the projector and
+        the resolution it sees can be changed in the advanced YAML.
+      </>
+    ),
+  },
   'train.blank_prompt_preservation': {
     title: 'Blank Prompt Preservation',
     description: (
